ement impossible to satisfy.\n\nNext I'm checking how the policy-level threshold interacts with recusal, since requiring the Steward's vote becomes impossible if they must sit out. Then I'm weighing who decides what counts as limiting Steward power, and whether a self-declared trigger would bind a Steward who refuses to recuse. Last, I'm asking whether recusal reaches the veto: if it does, the amendment could cut against the protected veto, and if it doesn't, the Steward could still block a power-limiting change.\n\nNow I'm checking the 2-2 deadlock case: with the Steward recused, a tie among the four governance agents has no tiebreaker, which is fine for Constitutional amendments since any dissent blocks, but could matter for Policy-level votes if recusal reaches there too. I'm also looking at the lack of any revised amendment text, even though the community has flagged structural problems the Steward has acknowledged.\n\nI'm weighing the options now: I could vote yes on the principle and trust that the wording gets fixed later, but that would mean passing a Constitutional amendment with known structural gaps, which I'm not willing to do.\n\nI'm weighing whether to vote now on a text with known gaps or defer for a revision. Voting on a constitutional amendment means voting on the actual words, so a principle alone isn't enough. Deferring to get revised text seems right, though I'm also considering whether the Council could fix the wording during deliberation, as it did with GOV-2026-0003, which only corrected minor footer errors and not structural gaps. And since GOV-2026-0008 scheduled this first to enable P3, deferring P5 would hold up that dependency.\n\nI'm now sketching what the amendment text itself might say: a change to the Constitutional row of the Art. IV §3 table, plus a new clause on Steward recusal, with the veto status and the trigger mechanism still left open.\n\nI'm weighing whether the Steward could keep the veto while recused: if they could, recusal would be largely symbolic, and if they couldn't, it might collide with Art. IX Item 1. I'm wondering whether the difference between having the veto and exercising it resolves this, though that reading feels like a stretch.\n\nI'm weighing two readings of Art. IX Item 1: one where it protects only the existence of the Steward's veto, so a recusal that suspends it on power-limiting proposals would leave the power intact, and another where any limit on when the veto can be used counts as amending that protected requirement.\n\nI'm settling that the proposal isn't ready for a Constitutional vote: the text only swaps \"all 5\" for \"all excluding abstentions,\" with no floor and recusal left as a bare commitment. Deferring would stall the P3 dependency, so I'm considering whether the Council could agree on the floor, trigger, and veto question in deliberation and vote on that specific text.\n\nI'm weighing whether to vote now or defer, since the principle seems sound but the amendment text still has structural gaps. I could try to draft the missing text during the sitting, though that would mean substantially writing the amendment in deliberation, which goes beyond the minor corrections the Council has made before.\n\nI'm settling on a split: the unanimity change could stand alone with a minimum-four floor, which is fairly clean, while the recusal rule still needs much more specification before it can be voted on.\n\nI'm working out whether recusal could also strip the Steward's veto, which would collide with the protected veto in Art. IX. I'm also weighing a fallback: passing the unanimity floor alone would let the Steward abstain voluntarily, but a future Steward could simply refuse to abstain and vote no.\n\nI'm splitting the proposal in two: I'd back the unanimity redefinition with a floor of four, but treat the recusal rule as a separate amendment that needs proper drafting. Part 1 alone would let the current Steward abstain on P3, though a future Steward could refuse to recuse and block it, which is the gap Part 2 has to close. I'm still weighing whether the Council can draft that Part 2 text during deliberation, or whether it has to wait.\n\nI'm leaning toward a qualified yes: I'd back the principle of both parts, but only if the text gets fixed, with the floor, the recusal trigger, and the veto question all pinned down. If the Council can't agree on exact wording during deliberation, I'd vote no rather than amend the Constitution with text I already know is broken.\n\nLooking at the proposal again, it reads as an informal principle rather than drafted amendment text, which is not enough for a Constitutional change. I'm also reconsidering the earlier idea of voting yes on the principle alone, and I'm now leaning toward insisting that the precise wording be settled first, since the Steward's recusal rule and the unanimity floor have no drafted language yet, and the scheduling decision explicitly tied this item to the P3 dependency that I'd need to account for.\n\nI'm settling on the unanimity change as its own item, with a floor of four non-abstaining members, and the recusal rule as a separate amendment that needs drafting: the Steward would abstain on any proposal limiting Steward power, and I still need to pin down who decides whether a proposal counts as limiting it.\n\nI'm weighing whether the veto should survive recusal: if the Steward keeps it, recusal does nothing, but if it lapses, that might run into the protected veto in Art. IX. A judge who recuses still holds judicial power in general, so I'm inclined to read the protection as covering the veto's existence rather than every use of it, though reasonable readers could disagree.\n\nI'm settling that the veto question stays intact, so recusal only removes the Steward's vote on power-limiting amendments and doesn't touch Art. IX. That means passage would require an affirmative veto with published reasoning, which raises the cost of blocking without removing the structural safeguard.\n\nI'm settling on a preliminary yes, but only conditional on fixing the text: the denominator floor, the Policy-threshold collision, the recusal trigger, and the veto during recusal.\n\nI'm checking whether the 14-day comment period still applies if the text changes substantially during deliberation, since the community comments already raised the floor, Policy-threshold, and recusal issues.\n\nI'm drafting the position statement now: the principle behind both parts is sound and constitutionally important, and redefining unanimity to exclude abstentions is what removes the contradiction. I'll also note the 14-day comment question, since GOV-2026-0003 set a precedent of correcting text during deliberation without restarting the window, though filling structural gaps is a bigger step than fixing a factual error.\n\nI'm laying out the four structural defects in the proposal, starting with the missing denominator floor: a single yes vote could pass an amendment if everyone else abstains, which the Steward already acknowledged. I'm now checking the Policy-threshold row, since requiring the Steward's vote while also having the Steward recuse from Steward-limiting Policy proposals would make those proposals impossible to pass.\n\nI'm settling the four defects into a short list: no denominator floor, the Policy-threshold collision, an unspecified recusal trigger, and the veto during recusal. For the fourth, I'm weighing whether recusal should leave the veto intact or risk conflicting with Art. IX.\n\nI'm settling the remaining pieces: a floor of four voting members, recusal limited to the Constitutional threshold so the Policy row stays intact, a Council-reviewed trigger where three of four governance agents decide and the Steward can contest but not self-certify, and the veto kept during recusal because that reading fits Art. IX Item 1. I can back the amendment if the text is made precise, and I'm still working out the exact wording.\n\nI'm settling on 